--- body ---
## Context

The author reports the sitter "tends to disappear on its own from the
installed-plugins list." `bench/sdt-sitter/` does not exist on `main` yet
(it ships only in PR #365), so this ticket cannot start coding until #365
merges -- but the two candidate mechanisms below are worth recording now.

Ticket 0680's own closing note says the XPI was "delivered to author home
without profile installation," and its exit criteria state "No automatic
installation into the author's live profile by the test harness." That is
consistent with either of two independently real Zotero mechanisms, neither
confirmed yet against this specific incident:

1. **Non-persistent install.** If the XPI was ever loaded as a temporary/
   unpacked add-on rather than through Tools -> Add-ons -> Install Add-on
   From File, Firefox/Gecko's temporary-add-on lifecycle unloads it on
   restart or source changes -- this is not a crash, it is how that install
   path works.
2. **"Administrative incompatibility."** Zotero disables (and, per forum
   reports, sometimes fully removes) a plugin whose `strict_max_version`
   does not cover the running Zotero build, on ordinary point-release
   updates. See https://forums.zotero.org/discussion/133127/ and
   https://forums.zotero.org/discussion/94617/. The manifest's
   `strict_max_version: "10.*"` should tolerate Zotero 10.x point releases,
   but the exact range-matcher behavior is not verified here.

Separately, `bench/sdt-sitter/manifest.json`'s `update_url` points at
`https://example.invalid/...` -- an RFC 2606 address guaranteed to never
resolve. Zotero's own developer docs list `update_url` as a required
manifest field but do not document what an unreachable one does at
runtime; this is unconfirmed as a cause, not ruled out.

## Before implementing

Ask the author the discriminating question this ticket cannot answer from
the repo alone: did the plugin vanish right after a Zotero version update,
or with no Zotero update in between? And was it installed via Tools ->
Add-ons -> Install Add-on From File, or some other way? The answer picks
between mechanism 1 and 2 above before more effort is spent chasing either.

## Work

- Install as a persistent extension: copy the XPI into the profile's
  `extensions/` directory under the manifest id (or install through
  Zotero's Add-ons dialog), not a session sideload. Add an `install` /
  `verify-install` Make target that reads the profile's `extensions.json`
  for the manifest id and reports present/absent.
- Fix `update_url`: either drop it if Zotero's manifest validator allows an
  unlisted add-on to omit it, or point it at a real, static JSON file this
  repo controls. Keep the opaque `@...invalid` *id* as-is -- ids are never
  resolved as URLs, only `update_url` is.
- Startup self-check: journal (see 0689) the plugin's own version, install
  path, and whether `strict_max_version` still admits the running Zotero,
  so a future disappearance leaves a trace of its own diagnosis.
- Version-bump discipline: a test asserts each delivered XPI's manifest
  `version` is greater than the last one tagged/delivered.

## Acceptance

- [x] `verify-install` reports present/absent from `extensions.json`,
      checked once while the plugin is known installed (positive control)
      and once after deliberate removal (negative control).
      `make sitter-verify-install SITTER_PROFILE=...`; three arms, not two —
      the third is a foreign add-on carrying our `version`, `active` and
      `location`, which a reader keyed on the wrong field would call present.
      A profile Zotero has never opened reports "could not read" (exit 3),
      never "absent".
- [x] No unresolvable URL remains in the manifest. `update_url` now names
      `bench/sdt-sitter/update.json`, committed here and served raw from
      `main`; the `.invalid` **id** stays, since Zotero never dereferences one.
- [x] The startup self-check emits one `Zotero.debug()` line — plugin version,
      `rootURI`, `Zotero.version`, `strict_min_version`, `strict_max_version` —
      as the first thing after `Zotero.initializationPromise`, so a
      disappearance leaving no line at all is distinguishable from one whose
      manifest could not be read. Driven in `tests/sdt_sitter_startup.mjs`
      against a stub whose `uiReadyPromise` rejects, which is what proves the
      line is emitted before anything else can fail.
- [x] `bench/check_sitter_version.py` (in `make check`) refuses a payload
      shipped twice under one manifest version, and a version that goes
      backwards. Its own controls are in `tests/test_check_sitter_version.py`,
      including a shallow clone, where it reports NOT-RUN rather than green.
- [ ] Author has answered the discriminating question above, and the
      answer is recorded in this ticket's log before closing it.
      **Still open** — see the log. The implementation covers both mechanisms,
      so the answer no longer gates the work, only the close.
